comments.body:
@englishmastiff2 You are receiving emails, every time someone posts, because you have automatically subscribed to this issue by posting in it.    You can unsubscribe.
This is an open issue and there will be lots of posts.  We need to communicate with the person who has asked for help.
Would you please stop responding to every post?

@JayJay265 It is possible the the receiver is not as sensitive as a phone.  I suspect that since the transmitter battery voltage is so low, the transmitted signal is not strong enough for the receiver.
But, this does not explain why the receiver's behavior changes when you use a different xDrip release!

I will be very interested in the behavior with a better transmitter battery.  For example, a Voltage B of 290.  Or, at least, 285.

@JayJay265 What is the status now?
Are all your settings matching the following?
https://github.com/Navid200/xDrip/wiki/G6-Recommended-Settings

@Navid200 
I have now used a different transmitter voltage B317. However, the problem has not changed.
![Screenshot_20210630-052933_xDrip+](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/80785292/123952232-ff3dbf00-d9a5-11eb-9ab1-9e92f76e0450.jpg)

@JayJay265
That can't be right.  Voltage A cannot be 220!
Something is very wrong with those values (vA < vB, R too large).

@Navid200 
![Screenshot_20210807-170435_xDrip+](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/80785292/128604615-e321e020-b6b6-4163-b6ae-54e7f4547d9e.jpg)
Found a Transmitter with Voltage 290. But nothing Change.

@JayJay265 So, with this transmitter, the receiver works fine with 2021.02.03 but the receiver stops working with 2021.02.04, while xDrip works fine either way, right?

Would you please also post a screenshot of the following page?
Settings -> G5/G6 Debug Settings
